# Django security

Narrows `python`'s security guidance to Django's injection-class sinks: views, forms/serializers, template-tag modules and settings. Applies only to the listed `.py` files; a non-request-facing module follows `python`'s `rules/security.mdc`.

## CSRF

- Leave `django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware` enabled for every state-changing view.
- Never add `@csrf_exempt` to make a request succeed unless the endpoint has an equivalent protection (a signed webhook signature, or a non-browser API authenticated by token, never session/cookie auth).
- A DRF view using `SessionAuthentication` keeps CSRF protection on unsafe methods; fix the client to send the token.

## Cross-site scripting (XSS)

- Keep Django's template auto-escaping on; do not disable it project-wide.
- Never apply `mark_safe()`/`|safe`/`format_html()`'s raw-string argument to a value that traces back to user input; reserve them for markup the application constructs and fully controls (e.g. a trusted CMS field rendered through a sanitizer).
- Build dynamic HTML with `format_html()`/`format_html_join()`, not concatenation or an f-string passed to `mark_safe()`.

## SQL injection

- Pass user-derived values to `.raw()` through `params` (`Model.objects.raw("... WHERE id = %s", [id])`), never via f-string/`%`/`.format()`.
- Treat `.extra()` as legacy; prefer `annotate`, `F()`, `Q()`, `Func` or a parameterized `.raw()` call.

## Settings and secrets

- `SECRET_KEY`: at least 50 characters, unique per environment, never the `django-insecure-` placeholder, never committed; read it from the environment/secret store.
- `DEBUG` is `False` in any settings module used in production.
- `ALLOWED_HOSTS` is a non-empty list of the real hostnames in production, never `["*"]`.
- Run `python manage.py check --deploy` before a production release.
